                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_coherence:     `<strong>That is the right diagnosis.</strong> This is a coherence failure, not a PR fire — the brand exists in the gap between what you say, what you do, and what stakeholders believe. The crisis lives in that gap, and so does the recovery. Now name which gap matters most.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp brand diagnosis.</strong> A coherence failure AND the say–do gap as the wound — promising “all-natural” while carrying an undisclosed additive. That divergence is exactly what 2.4 million people are now reading. You cannot close it with a slogan; you close it with substance.`,
                    coach_r1_prfire:        `<strong>“A bad week we ride out” is how a perception problem becomes a reputation collapse.</strong> A corporate brand is what stakeholders believe it to be — and right now they believe the worst. Treating it as noise leaves the underlying say–do gap open for the journalist to find.`,
                    coach_r1_sales:         `<strong>Winning the shelf back is a symptom, not the cause.</strong> The chains delisted because trust broke; chase the relisting before you fix the coherence gap and the same allegation pulls you off the shelf again next month.`,
                    coach_r1_legal:         `<strong>Reaching for the lawyers before your own results are in is premature defiance.</strong> If your lab later confirms a deviation, you will have called a true claim “defamatory” — and a brand is what people believe. You just gave them a reason to believe the worst.`,
                    coach_r1_image:         `<strong>Correcting the image without closing the say–do gap is cosmetic.</strong> You can change what people believe for a week, but the undisclosed additive is still there. The gap will resurface the moment a journalist reads the label on air.`,
                    coach_r1_denial:        `<strong>“No real gap, the claim is false” is denial.</strong> The additive is real and undisclosed — that is a genuine say–do gap, permitted or not. Pretend it isn’t there and your entire response is built on a foundation that won’t hold.`,
                    coach_r1_allperception: `<strong>Calling this 100% perception denies the real gap.</strong> The additive is permitted but it was never disclosed — that is a substance-and-disclosure problem, not just viral noise. Misdiagnose the mix and you will spend on messaging when you needed reformulation or disclosure.`,
                    coach_r2_reframe:       `<strong>Reframing to “safe, transparent, trusted” is coherent — if you make it true.</strong> Moving from an absolute “all-natural” claim to a disclosure-led promise closes the say–do gap honestly. Now the substance has to match the new words, or you have just changed the slogan.`,
                    coach_r2_defend:        `<strong>Defending “all-natural” by reformulating is the high-integrity path.</strong> Removing the additive makes the 35-year claim true again — slow and costly, but it is the only way to keep that exact promise without lying. Make sure the timeline is credible to stakeholders.`,
                    coach_r2_hold:          `<strong>Holding “all-natural” and contesting the facts keeps the say–do gap wide open.</strong> You are now defending a claim the product doesn’t support. If the lab confirms anything, you have layered a denial on top of the original gap — the most dangerous place to be.`,
                    coach_r2_reposition:    `<strong>An overnight pivot to “science-forward food-tech” is a panic rebrand.</strong> Loyal consumers bought you for “family tradition since 1991” — relaunch as something else and the press calls it exactly what it is. You can evolve the promise; you cannot abandon the equity you are trying to save.`,
                    coach_r2_reformulate:   `<strong>Reformulation closes the say–do gap at the substance level.</strong> That is prior to and more important than any message — once the product matches the promise, every Naira you spend communicating is spent on something true.`,
                    coach_r2_relabel:       `<strong>Relabel-and-disclose closes the gap honestly.</strong> Keeping a permitted additive but declaring it clearly turns a hidden divergence into a transparent fact. It is faster than reformulating and it makes the new “transparent” identity credible.`,
                    coach_r2_nochange:      `<strong>“Purely a communication exercise” is Common Error number one.</strong> You are about to spend money broadcasting an identity the product still contradicts. Messaging cannot repair a broken say–do gap — change the substance or change the claim first.`,
                    coach_r2_lowheritage:   `<strong>Walking away from 35 years of meaning confuses the very people you need.</strong> Reputation is accumulated over time; a near-zero link to “Nourishing Nigerian families” reads as a panic rebrand and forfeits the equity that was your only real asset going in.`,
                    coach_r3_proof:         `<strong>Weighting the budget toward proof is the trust-crisis play.</strong> Independent certification and a traceability microsite let stakeholders verify rather than take your word. In a crisis of belief, verifiable proof outperforms volume every time.`,
                    coach_r3_reachonly:     `<strong>You put the budget into reach and skipped the proof.</strong> Paid advertising and reposts shout “trust us” louder; certification and traceability give people a reason to. You bought maximum volume and zero evidence — in a trust crisis, that is the most expensive mistake on the menu.`,
                    coach_r3_opengap:       `<strong>You are spending heavily on advertising while the say–do gap is still open.</strong> You are paying to broadcast the exact inconsistency that caused this. When the journalist reads the label on air, what happens to every Naira you just spent?`,
                    coach_r3_reassuring:    `<strong>“Nothing to worry about” before your own results are in is a hostage to fortune.</strong> Reassurance without proof reads as dismissal; if the lab later confirms a deviation, the calm message becomes the clip that runs against you.`,
                    coach_r3_defiant:       `<strong>Going defiant before your results are in is premature defiance.</strong> “Malicious false claims” and lawyers’ letters are a reversal waiting to happen — if your own lab confirms a deviation, you have lied on top of the original gap. Calibrate to what you actually know.`,
                    coach_r3_incoherent:    `<strong>Different messages to different audiences is the evasion signal.</strong> Tell consumers “nothing to worry about,” retailers “a serious issue,” and NAFDAC “we’ve begun reformulation,” and those three statements end up side by side online. Stakeholders build your reputation from the whole picture.`,
                    coach_r4_proof:         `<strong>Institutionalising proof is the durable repositioning.</strong> Making certification, on-pack disclosure and traceability the standard turns this crisis into the reason customers trust you again — reputation rebuilt with proof points, not slogans.`,
                    coach_r4_heritage:      `<strong>Re-anchoring heritage — now evidenced — preserves the equity.</strong> Recommitting to “Nourishing Nigerian families,” backed by the certified result and disclosure, evolves the 35-year promise without erasing it. Consistency over time is what reputation is made of.`,
                    coach_r4_victory:       `<strong>Declaring victory and stopping is the vindication trap.</strong> The result may be clean, but the tracker is still at 51 and two retailers haven’t relisted. Being right is not the same as being trusted again — recovery is a separate task from exoneration.`,
                    coach_r4_pivot:         `<strong>A hard pivot now is a panic rebrand under fire.</strong> Abandoning the promise at the moment of maximum scrutiny tells loyal consumers and the press you have something to run from. Reposition by evolving the meaning, not by erasing it.`,
                    coach_r4_cleanstop:     `<strong>The result is clean — and you stopped.</strong> But 60% of the people who saw the video never saw your clean result, and the shelf is still half empty. You proved you were right and left the trust unrepaired. Vindication is not recovery.`,
                    coach_r4_breachlied:    `<strong>Your own lab confirmed a deviation — after you over-claimed.</strong> You called a true allegation false, or kept the gap hidden, and now the facts have caught up. A brand is what people believe; you handed them every reason to believe the worst.`,
                    coach_r4_breachhonest:  `<strong>A confirmed deviation — but you were honest and closed the gap early.</strong> Because you didn’t over-claim and you disclosed or reformulated, the deviation reads as a company that found and fixed a problem, not one that lied about it. That is calibrated response under uncertainty.`,
                    coach_r4_facedown:      `<strong>That faces it down with evidence.</strong> A coherent repositioning plus leading the board and the journalist with the certified result, the traceability, the relisted shelves and honest KPI projections — that is a story that survives either lab outcome and the press piece.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Restating the mission and claiming full vindication is spin, and I’ll write it that way.</strong> The claim under question was the mission itself — repeating it instead of evidencing it is exactly what an exposé needs.`,
                    coach_r4_silence:       `<strong>No comment hands me the narrative.</strong> The certified result doesn’t speak for itself when most consumers never saw it. Silence reads as guilt — the story runs without your side of it.`,

