                    insight_rebuilt:        `Reputation rebuilt: you treated the brand as socially constructed and realigned substance, identity and image as one story — closed the say–do gap, bought proof over reach, kept one message across stakeholders, and repositioned without erasing 35 years of equity. The tracker recovered, the shelf came back, and the journalist’s story had no gap to exploit.`,
                    insight_exposed:        `Exposé confirmed: an open say–do gap, a panic rebrand, or over-claiming before the result handed the journalist the story. The reputation built since 1991 was punctured because image diverged sharply from identity and you didn’t close the gap.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn’t fully realign substance, identity and image. The three still tell slightly different stories — enough for stakeholders and the press to read the seam and withhold the benefit of the doubt.`,
                    insight_breach:         `The lab confirmed a minor deviation. Whether that sank or held you came down to Round 2 and 3: honesty and an early-closed say–do gap survive a deviation; defiance and denial do not. Deciding under uncertainty is the whole test.`,
                    insight_clean:          `The lab came back clean — but a clean result is not recovery. Reputation repair is a separate task from factual exoneration: the consumers who never saw the result and the retailers who haven’t relisted are won back with proof and presence, not a press release saying “we were right.”`,
                    insight_saydo:          `The say–do gap was never closed. Promising one thing while the product does another is a structural inconsistency no amount of communication repairs — the additive should have been reformulated out or transparently disclosed before a Naira was spent on messaging.`,
                    insight_overclaim:      `You over-claimed before your own results were in. Contesting the allegation as false or staying defiant created the risk of a humiliating reversal — calibrated response under uncertainty means not asserting more than you can yet prove.`,
